\[c_{1}=\text{sgn}\left[\sin\left(q_{2x}\right)\right],\quad-\frac{\sqrt{3}\pi} {2}\leq q_{2x}\leq\frac{\sqrt{3}\pi}{2}. \tag{5}\]

Interestingly, the Chern number depends only on the spin wave vectors \(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(q_{2x},0\right)\) -- the azimuthal angle. However, it does not mean that when \(\vec{q}_{1}=0\) the topological effect is present; it is absent [63]. Physically, it means for topological effects to occur some inclination of spin vector is necessary, which is related to nonzero effective magnetic field [64].

#### iii.1.2 Chern number for \(S=2,3,\dots\)

For higher spin \(S=2,3,\dots\) one will need to expand the terms containing \(\vec{q}_{n}\) and \(\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}\) using binomial theorem [see App. C]. In Fig. 6 we plotted the dependence of \(\vec{q}_{n}\) and \(\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}\) on \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\). We observe that the value of \(\vec{q}_{n}\) and \(\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}\) are always positive and less than unity for most part of the phase space \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\). Hence, one can neglect the higher powers of \(\vec{q}_{n}\) and \(\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}\) and keeping terms only upto first power in the binomial expansion. The resulting Hamiltonian is [see App. C for derivation]:

\[\mathcal{H}_{0}\approx-2t_{2}\sum\limits_{n}\omega_{n}^{S}\left[1+\frac{S \vec{q}_{n}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{b}_{n}\right]\times\]

\[\left\{\cos S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\cos\vec{k}\vec{b}_{n}+2S\sin S\vec{q}_{2} \vec{b}_{n}\sum\limits_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\rho(\vec{k}^{\prime})\cos\left(\vec {k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{x}\approx+t_{1}\sum\limits_{n}\omega_{n}^{\prime S}\left[1-\frac {S\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{n}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{y}\approx+t_{1}\sum\limits_{n}\omega_{n}^{\prime S}\left[1-\frac {S\vec{q}_{n}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\sin\vec{k}\vec{a}_{n}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{z}\approx-2t_{2}\sum\limits_{n}\omega_{n}^{S}\left[1+\frac{S\vec{q }_{n}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{b}_{n}\right]\times\]

\[\left\{-\sin S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{n}+2S\cos S\vec{q}_{2 }\vec{b}_{n}\sum\limits_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\rho(\vec{k}^{\prime})\sin\left(\vec {k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}. \tag{6}\]

Comparing Eq. (4) and Eq. (6) one can see the extra factor of \(S\) appears in different terms of the Hamiltonian. For Eq. (6) the topological condition, \(\mathcal{H}_{x}=\mathcal{H}_{y}=0\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\neq 0\), is satisfied simultaneously at \(\vec{K}=\left(\pm\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) for \(\vec{q}_{1}=\left(2q_{1x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) and \(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(2q_{2x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\). The Chern number is [see App. E.2 for derivation]:

\[c_{1}=\text{sign}\left[\left(1+\frac{S\vec{q}_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right) \left(\sin Sq_{2x}-2S\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\right)\right] \tag{7}\]

Here, we introduced the symbol \(\epsilon\equiv\sum\limits_{K^{\prime}}\rho(k^{\prime})\sin\left(\pi/2+\vec{k}^ {\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right)\); it's value is always \(\ll 1\) over whole \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\) phase space [see Fig. 17]. One can see that, now the Chern number depends also on the \(q_{1x}\) -- the polar angle; for \(S=1\) it was absent. In fact, one can recover the Chern number for \(S=1\), Eq. (5), from Eq. (7). It can be seen as follows. For \(S=1\) the \(|g_{2}\cos 2q_{1x}/2|\) will always be less than unity (as \(0\leq g_{2}\leq 1\)).In result, the whole term \(\left(1+\frac{S\vec{q}_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right)\) will always be positive irrespective of \(q_{1x}\). Therefore this term will not have any affect on the \(c_{1}\), and one can drop this from Eq. (7). As mentioned before the \(\epsilon\ll 1\); the only reason for including it in Eq. (7), is that for large \(S\) the term, \(2S\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\), might be larger than \(\sin Sq_{2x}\). However, for \(S=1\) the \(2\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\) will never be greater than \(\sin Sq_{2x}\), apart from some narrow regions of \(q_{1x}\)-\(q_{2x}\) phase space. Hence, we will drop the \(2\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\) term also in Eq. (7). Therefore we recovered the Eq. (5).

### Chern number for Half-Integer Spins

Finding a general analytical expression of Chern number for \(S=1/2\) is little bit tricky, as Fourier transform of the terms,

\[\hat{\mathcal{F}}\left[1-g_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{ i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2}, \tag{8}\] \[\hat{\mathcal{F}}\left[1+g_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{ i}+\frac{\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2},\]

are not available. Therefore, one need to find the Hamiltonian in the limiting cases of \(g_{n}\) and \(g_{n}^{\prime}\), and then make the necessary conclusions. We can have four limiting case of \(g_{n}\) and \(g_{n}^{\prime}\) in different combinations. The four cases and corresponding Hamiltonian are given in Tab. 2. Observing these Hamiltonian we will see that they are analogous to the Eq. (4). Meaning, the term containing \(\vec{k}\) remains same. The only difference is the factors involving \(g_{n}\) and \(g_{n}^{\prime}\). Hence, their Chern number will be given by Eq. (5).

The calculation for Hamiltonian for large half-integer \(S\) is given in App. 2. The main idea is to represent:

\[\left[1-g_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a }_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2}\approx\left[1-g_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2} \tag{9}\] \[\times\left[1-g_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+ \frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{(S-1/2)}.\]

Then use the approximations for \(S=1/2\) (App. 1) and higher integer \(S\) (App. 2) to find the Fourier transform of the resulting expression. The calculation procedure is described in App. 2. The resulting Hamiltonians will also be analogous to the Eq. (6). Therefore the Chern number will be given by Eq. (7).

## III Discussion

### General formula for Chern number

From the above discussions we can conclude that Eq. (7) gives the Chern number for both integer or half-integer spins. In Fig. 7 we plotted the Chern number dependence on \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\) for spin upto \(S=3\). Near \(q_{1x}\approx 0\) and \(q_{2x}\) the Chern number is not defined. The \(q_{1x}=0\) physically represent the spin vector pointing towards the north pole, and \(q_{2x}=0\) represent the rotation of the spin only along some fixed longitude of Bloch sphere; both these cases have ill defined Chern number. One can observe that apart from some small discrepancies in most part of the figure the Chern number depends only on the azimuthal modulating vector \(q_{2x}\). Hence Eq. (5) can be used as an approximated formula for Chern number. Physically, the dependence of Chern number on \(Sq_{2x}\) can be understood as the amount of effective magnetic field threading through three lattice points locally [54]. The effective local magnetic field through three lattice points (\(i,j,k\)) is \(\text{B}_{eff}=\vec{S}_{i}\cdot\left[\vec{S}_{j}\times\vec{S}_{k}\right]\) is proportional to the \(S^{3}\), hence, with increase in \(S\) the threaded magnetic field also increases.

