### Comparison with Haldane model

If compared to the Haldane model [55], then the topological property of the Haldane model was controlled by the sublattice symmetry breaking onsite potential (\(M\)) and the phase accumulation of electrons due to NNN hopping (defined as \(\phi\) in Ref. [55]). In our case the factor \(Sq_{2x}\) plays the analogous phase accumulation role due to NNN hopping. In fact we can phenomenologically insert additional sublattice hopping potential \(M\) into the \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) of Eq. (2). The Chern number in this case is:

\[c_{1}=\] \[-\frac{\text{sign}\left[M+2t_{2}\left(1+\frac{Sq_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x }\right)\left(\sin Sq_{2x}-2S\varepsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\right)\right]}{2}. \tag{11}\]

In Fig. 9 we plotted the Chern number dependence on \(M/t_{2}\) and \(q_{2x}\) for \(S=3\); the \(q_{1x}=\pi/4\) is kept constant.

### Minimum Energy

To understand the thermodynamics of the system we calculate the total internal energy of the system through equation:

\[U_{\text{internal}}=\sum_{k\in BZ}E(k)f\left[E(k),\mu\right] \tag{12}\]

\(E(k)\) is the energy of the lowest band defined as \(\mathcal{H}_{0}\) - \(\sqrt{\mathcal{H}_{x}^{2}+\mathcal{H}_{y}^{2}+\mathcal{H}_{z}^{2}}\) in Eq. \(f\left[E(k),\mu\right]\) is the Fermi-Dirac distribution; \(k_{B}=8.6\times 10^{-5}\) eV K\({}^{-1}\) is the Boltzman constant; \(T\) is the temperature. In our calculation we take \(T\approx 0\) K. The summation in Eq. (12) is taken over the whole Brillouin zone. In Fig. 10 we plotted the \(U_{\text{internal}}\) for different \(S\). In most cases the lowest energy has an anti-ferromagnetic configuration, which was expected, as the original Hamiltonian, Eq. (117) will have lowest energy when the dot product of the \(\vec{S}_{i}\cdot\vec{S}_{j}\) will have negative sign.

