operators appear as a generators of the \(su(2|1)\) superalgebras. As a result charge and spin degrees of freedom can be represented as product of the \(SU(2|1)\) supergroup. This is applicable for particles described by the \(S=1/2\), however, for arbitrary spin \(S>1/2\) one will use the \(su(2)\) algebra. In the \(SU(2)\) formalism the required theory is constructed under the condition that the background spin field affects the fermion hopping without breaking the global symmetry. The \(su(2)\) coherent states (CS) have these properties, as the electron hopping factor is affected only due to CS overlap factors [101; 102]. In condensed matter it is analogous to the Peierls phase factor generated in an external magnetic field. It is the vector potential generated by the non-collinear chiral spin textures [64]. Physically it can be thought of fictitious magnetic field through a plaquette. In field theory it is the same as the emergent artificial gauge field generated by the \(U(1)\) local connection one-form of the spin \(U(1)\) complex line bundle. It provides a covariant (geometric) quantization of a spin [103]. In this approach the underlying base space appears as a classical spin phase space. It is a two sphere \(S^{2}\) which can be mapped to a complex projective space \(CP^{1}\), endowed with a set of local coordinates \(\left(z,\bar{z}\right)\). In this case quantum spin is represented as the section \(\left|z\right\rangle\) of the principle (monopole) line bundle \(P\left(CP^{1},U(1)\right)\). The local connection of the bundle is \(a^{(0)}=i\left\langle z\right|d\left|z\right\rangle\); \(d\) is the exterior derivative.

Physically, we start from the lattice Kondo-model:

\[\begin{split} H=-\sum_{ij\sigma}&[t_{ij}+J\,S(S+1 )\,\,\delta_{ij}]c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}c_{j\sigma}\\ &+J\sum_{i}\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{i}\cdot(c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma} \vec{\sigma}_{\sigma\sigma^{\prime}}c_{i\sigma^{\prime\prime}}).\end{split} \tag{10}\]

Here \(c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}\) (\(c_{i\sigma}\)) is the electron creation (annihilation) operator with the spin \(\sigma\) on site \(i\); \(J>0\) is the exchange coupling constant; \(\vec{\sigma}\) is the vector of the _Pauli_ spin matrices; \(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{i}\) is the nuclear spin operator at \(i\)-th site. The extra \(J\) dependent term \(J\,S(S+1)\,\,\delta_{ij}\) introduced in the hopping parameter is to make sure a finite \(J\rightarrow\infty\) limit [57]. Under the mean field approximation one can represent the nuclear spin operator as product of the localized spin magnitude (\(S\)) and their direction \(\left\langle\vec{n}_{i}\right\rangle\): \(\left\langle\vec{\mathbf{S}}_{i}\right\rangle=S\cdot\vec{n}_{i}\). In the large Kondo limit \(J\rightarrow\infty\) the Hubbard model goes into the \(U\rightarrow\infty\) limit (strongly correlated electronic system) [57]:

\[H\approx-\sum_{i,j,\sigma}t_{ij}\vec{c}^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}\vec{c}_{j\sigma}. \tag{11}\]

Here \(\vec{c}_{i\sigma}=c_{i\sigma}(1-n_{i\sigma})\) is the constrained electron operator; \(n_{i\sigma}=c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}c_{i\sigma}\) is the number operator of the complementary spin. The constraint operator as explained above can be dynamically factorized into the spinless charge fermionic field \(f_{i}\) (holons) and spinfull bosonic \(z_{i}\) fields (spinons) [59; 41]. It can be seen that as long as the fermionic field satisfy the condition \(f_{i}^{2}\equiv 0\), the local no double occupancy of strongly correlated electron is satisfied rigorously. Here, the holons acquire the band structure of their own; it is usual behavior for fractionalized electrons [104]. The spinons are handled by mean-field treatment.

The necessary theory was given by the authors recently [41], hence, here we briefly derive the required Hamiltonian. The high spin CS theory is constructed from the fundamental \(S=1/2\) representation:

\[\left|z\right\rangle=\left(1+\left|z\right|^{2}\right)^{-S}\mathrm{e}^{z \hat{S}^{-}}\left|S\right\rangle. \tag{12}\]

Here \(\left|S\right\rangle\) is the highest spin-\(S\)\(su(2)\) state; \(\hat{S}^{-}\) is the spin lowering operator. The \(S\)-dependent partition function will be:

\[Z=\int D\mu\left(z,f\right)\ \exp\mathcal{A}. \tag{13}\]

The measure \(D\mu\left(z,f\right)\) is:

\[D\mu\left(z,f\right)=\prod_{i,t}\frac{S}{\pi i}\frac{d\bar{z}_{i}\left(t \right)d\bar{z}_{i}\left(t\right)}{\left(1+\left|z_{i}\right|^{2}\right)^{2}}d \bar{f}_{i}(t)df_{i}(t). \tag{14}\]

Here \(z_{i}\) keeps track of the spin and complex, while \(f_{i}\) keeps track of charge and is a _Grassman_ variable. The effective action \(\mathcal{A}\) is defined as:

\[\mathcal{A}=\sum_{i}\int\limits_{0}^{\beta}\left[i\dot{a}_{i}^{(0)}-\bar{f}_{ i}\left(\partial_{t}+ia_{i}^{(0)}\right)f_{i}\right]dt-\int\limits_{0}^{\beta}H\ dt. \tag{15}\]

Here, \(ia_{i}^{(0)}\) is the \(u(1)\)-valued connection one-form of the magnetic monopole bundle as a _spin kinetic_ term:

\[ia_{i}^{(0)}=-\left\langle z\right|\partial_{t}\left|z\right\rangle=S\frac{ \hat{\bar{z}}z-\bar{z}\hat{z}}{1+\left|z\right|^{2}}. \tag{16}\]

It is analogous to the Berry connection. The Hamiltonian in \(\mathcal{A}\) can be written as:

\[H=-\sum_{ij}t_{ij}\bar{f}_{i}f_{j}\mathrm{e}^{ia_{ji}}+H.c.+\mu\sum_{i}\bar{f}_ {i}f_{i}, \tag{17}\]

where,

\[a_{ij}=-i\log\left\langle z_{i}\middle|z_{j}\right\rangle,\ \left\langle z_{i} \middle|z_{j}\right\rangle=\frac{\left(1+\bar{z}_{i}z_{j}\right)^{2S}}{\left(1 +\left|z_{j}\right|^{2}\right)^{S}\left(1+\left|z_{i}\right|^{2}\right)^{S}} . \tag{18}\]

Under a _global SU(2)_ rotation

\[z_{i}\rightarrow\frac{uz_{i}+v}{-\bar{v}z_{i}+\bar{u}} \tag{19}\]

the phase will be:

\[a_{i}^{(0)}\to a_{i}^{(0)}-\partial_{t}\theta_{i},\quad a_{ij}\to a_{ ij}+\theta_{j}-\theta_{i}. \tag{20}\]

Here,

\[\theta_{i}=iS\log\left(\frac{v\bar{z}_{i}+u}{\bar{v}z_{i}+\bar{u}}\right); \quad\begin{bmatrix}u&v\\ -\bar{v}&\bar{u}\end{bmatrix}\in SU(2). \tag{21}\]

The effective action \(\mathcal{A}\) remains unchanged under \(SU(2)\) transformation of the \(z_{i}\) in conjunction with \(U(1)\) transformation of the \(f_{i}\rightarrow\mathrm{e}^{i\,\theta_{i}}f_{i}\). The real and imaginary part of the \(a_{ji}\) is defined as:

\[a_{ji}=\phi_{ji}+i\chi_{ji};\;\phi_{ji}=\bar{\phi}_{ji};\;\chi_{ji}=\bar{\chi}_{ ji}. \tag{113}\]

The \(\phi_{ji}\) and \(\chi_{ji}\) are defined as

\[\begin{split}\phi_{ji}&=iS\log\frac{1+\bar{z}_{i}z_ {j}}{1+\bar{z}_{j}z_{i}}\\ &=iS\log\frac{\left(S+S_{i}^{z}\right)\,\left(S+S_{j}^{z}\right)+ S_{i}^{-}S_{j}^{+}}{\left(S+S_{i}^{z}\right)\,\left(S+S_{j}^{z}\right)+S_{j}^{-}S_{i}^{+}} ;\\ \chi_{ji}&=-S\log\frac{\left(1+\bar{z}_{i}z_{j} \right)\,\left(1+\bar{z}_{j}z_{i}\right)}{\left(1+|z_{i}|^{2}\right)\,\left(1+| z_{j}|^{2}\right)}\\ &=-S\log\left(\frac{\bar{S}_{i}\cdot\bar{S}_{j}}{2S^{2}}+\frac{1 }{2}\right).\end{split} \tag{114}\]

Here, \(\bar{S}_{i}\) stands for the CS symbols of the \(su\left(2\right)\) generators [59]. The corresponding values are:

\[S_{i}^{z}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{1-|z|^{2}}{1+|z|^{2}},\;S_{i}^{+}=\frac{z}{1+|z|^{2 }},\;S_{i}^{-}=\frac{\bar{z}}{1+|z|^{2}}. \tag{115}\]

There is a one-to-one correspondence between the \(su\left(2\right)\) generators and their CS symbols [61]. Under \(SU\left(2\right)\)_global_ rotation \(\chi_{ji}\) remains intact, however, \(\phi_{ji}\) transforms as:

\[\phi_{ji}\rightarrow\phi_{ji}+\theta_{i}-\theta_{j}. \tag{116}\]

This transformation is analogous to gauge fixing by choosing a specific rotational covariant frame. The dynamical fluxes do not depend on the chosen covariant frame. Substituting Eq. (114) in the dynamical Hamiltonian Eq. (108) we get:

\[H=-\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}\bar{f}_{i}f_{j}e^{i\phi_{ji}}\left(\frac{\bar{S}_{i}\cdot \bar{S}_{j}}{2S^{2}}+\frac{1}{2}\right)^{S}+\mu\sum_{i}\bar{f}_{i}f_{i}. \tag{117}\]

Physically, this represents the interaction of the underlying spin field and itinerant spinless fermions.

## Appendix B Solution of Hamiltonian on a bipartite lattice

We use the Hamiltonian Eq. (117) with the spin texture Eq. (1) to analyze the topological properties of the two band materials, as they are the simplest one. We take a bipartite lattice \(L\) with two sub-lattices \(A\) and \(B\): \(L=A\oplus B\). The charge and spin degrees of freedom on \(A\) sub-lattice are \(f_{i}\) and \(z_{i}\) respectively. For convenience on sub-lattice \(B\) we can define:

\[f_{i}\to f_{i}\mathrm{e}^{i\,\theta_{i}^{(0)}},\quad z_{i} \rightarrow-\frac{1}{\bar{z}_{i}};\qquad i\in B. \tag{118}\]

Where \(\theta_{i}^{(0)}\equiv\theta_{i}|_{u=0,v=1}\). Under these transformations the \(\chi_{ji}\) remains unchanged, while the \(\phi_{ji}\rightarrow\phi_{ij}+\theta_{j}^{(0)}-\theta_{i}^{(0)}\). This transformation makes the calculation easier, while conserving the form of \(a_{ij}\) in Eq. (111) under _global_\(SU\left(2\right)\) rotation. The coherent states symbols in Eq. (115) also changes from \(\bar{S}_{i}\rightarrow-\bar{S}_{i}\). For bipartite lattice the total Hamiltonian will contain three parts depending on hopping of electrons: (i) \(A\to A\), (ii) \(B\to B\), (iii) \(A\to B\). For \(A\to A\) (\(i,j\in A\)) the \(\chi_{ji}\) is:

\[\begin{split}\mathrm{e}^{\chi_{ij}}&=w_{ij}^{S} \left[1+\mathpzc{z}_{ij}\cos\bar{q}_{1}\left(2\vec{r}_{i}+\vec{r}_{ij}\right) \right]^{S};\\ & w_{ij}\equiv\frac{1}{2}+\left(\frac{1}{4}+\frac{\cos\bar{q}_{2 }\vec{r}_{ij}}{4}\right)\cos\bar{q}_{1}\vec{r},\\ & g_{ij}\equiv\left[\left(\frac{1}{4}-\frac{\cos\bar{q}_{2}\vec{ r}_{ij}}{4}\right)\,\left/\omega_{ij}\right]\,.\end{split} \tag{119}\]

Here we defined \(\vec{r}_{ij}=\vec{r}_{j}-\vec{r}_{i}\). The values of \(\omega_{ij}\) and \(\mathpzc{z}_{ij}\) are bounded \(\in\left[0,1\right]\), and constant for a given \(\vec{q}_{1}\) and \(\vec{q}_{2}\). The \(\phi_{ji}\) is:

\[\begin{split}&\phi_{ji}=2S\,\mathrm{atan}\,\left[\frac{1}{\bar{ \varkappa}\left(r\right)\,\csc\bar{q}_{2}\vec{r}_{ij}+\cot\bar{q}_{2}\vec{r}_{ij} }\right];\\ &\bar{\varkappa}\left(r\right)\equiv\\ &\frac{2+\cos\bar{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{ij}+\left[4\cos\frac{\bar{q}_{1 }\vec{r}_{ij}}{2}+1\right]\,\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{r}_{ij}}{2} \right)}{\cos\bar{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{ij}-\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{r}_{ij}}{2} \right)}.\end{split} \tag{120}\]

The \(\phi_{ji}\) is a periodic, but bounded function. \(\bar{\varkappa}\left(r\right)\) is also a periodic function which depends only on the polar angle \(\vec{q}_{1}\). For \(B\to B\) the \(\chi_{ij}\) remains same. However, the \(\phi_{ji}\big{|}_{i,j\in B}\rightarrow-\phi_{ji}\big{|}_{i,j\in A}+2S\vec{q}_{2 }\vec{r}_{ij}\)[105]. Explicitly,

\[\phi_{ji}=2S\,\mathrm{atan}\,\left[\frac{1}{\bar{\varkappa}\left(r\right)\, \csc\bar{q}_{2}\vec{r}_{ij}+\cot\bar{q}_{2}\vec{r}_{ij}}\right]+2S\vec{q}_{2} \vec{r}_{ij}. \tag{121}\]

