\[\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2} \tag{46}\] \[\approx\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\frac{\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}}{ 2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right].\]

Hence, when \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}\ll 1\) and \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\ll 1\) the complete Hamiltonian can be approximated as:

\[\mathcal{H}_{0}\approx-2t_{2}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}\left[1+\frac{ \mathpzc{G}_{n}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{b}_{n}\right]\times \tag{47}\] \[\left\{\cos\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\cos\vec{k}\vec{b}_{n} +\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sum_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\mathpzc{\rho}( \vec{k}^{\prime})\cos\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}\] \[\mathcal{H}_{x}\approx+t_{1}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}^{\prime}\left[1- \frac{\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\cos\vec{k} \vec{a}_{n}\] \[\mathcal{H}_{y}\approx+t_{1}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}^{\prime}\left[1- \frac{\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\sin \vec{k}\vec{a}_{n}\] \[\mathcal{H}_{z}\approx-2t_{2}\sum_{n}w_{n}\left[1+\frac{ \mathpzc{G}_{n}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{b}_{n}\right]\times\] \[\left(-\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{n} +\cos\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sum_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\mathpzc{\rho}( \vec{k}^{\prime})\sin\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right).\]

When \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}\approx 1\) one first need to approximate \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}=1-\gamma_{n}\); where \(\gamma_{n}\) is a small quantity. Further substituting this in Eq. (45) we will get:

\[\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1+\mathpzc{G}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2} \tag{48}\] \[\approx\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\frac{\gamma_{n}}{2\sqrt{2}} \ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\right]=\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\frac{1- \mathpzc{G}_{n}}{2\sqrt{2}}\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\right];\] \[\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\equiv\frac{\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\right)}{\cos\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_ {i}+\frac{\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\right)}.\]

Eq. (48) is applicable only when the condition \(1+\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\right)\gg\gamma\) is satisfied. Besides, the function \(\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\) is defined everywhere except in the vicinity of \(\mathpzc{\tilde{q}}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}\approx\pi/2-(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{b}_{n}/2)\). In Fig. 16 we plot the function \(\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\) for \(\gamma=0.1\); with increase in \(\gamma\) the shape of the function does not change much. In fact one can approximate this function using Dirac comb, i.e. we can write the function as summation of the two opposite valued Dirac combs shifted by \(\pi\) compared to each other:

\[\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\approx\sum_{n}\delta(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}-2n \pi)-\sum_{n}\delta(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}-(2n+1)\pi) \tag{49}\]

Here, \(\delta\) is the Dirac delta function. Hence, explicitly we will have:

\[\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\frac{\gamma}{2\sqrt{2}}\ell(\vec{q}_{1} \vec{r}_{i})\right] \tag{50}\] \[=\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\frac{\gamma}{2\sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n} \delta(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}-2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\delta(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}-(2 n+1)\pi)\right\}\right]\]

The fourier transform of Dirac comb is known. The Fourier transform of the first part of the Eq. (50):

\[\mathcal{F}\left[\sum_{n}\delta(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}-2n\pi)\right]=\sum_{n}2 \cos\left(2n\pi\right) \tag{51}\]

Similarly we can write the Fourier transform of the second part of the Eq. (50). When \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\approx 1\) we proceed the same way; defining \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}=1-\gamma_{n}^{\prime}\) we will have:

\[\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1-\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2} \tag{52}\] \[\approx\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1+\frac{\gamma_{n}^{\prime}}{2\sqrt {2}}\ell(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\right]=\hat{\mathpzc{F}}\left[1+\frac{1- \mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}}{2\sqrt{2}}\ell^{\prime}(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}) \right];\] \[\mathpzc{\rho}^{\prime}(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\equiv\frac{\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)}{\sin\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_ {i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)}.\]

As in Eq. (48), Eq. (52) is applicable when the condition \(1+\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}-\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\gg\gamma\) is satisfied. The function \(\mathpzc{\rho}^{\prime}(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i})\) is defined everywhere except in the vicinity of \(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{r}_{i}\approx\pi/2-(\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}/2)\). The Fourier transform of Eq. (52) is found by the same way as in Eq. (50). Substituting the Fourier transforms of Eq. (50) and Eq. (52) in Eq. (2) we will get the Hamiltonian:

\[\mathcal{H}_{0}=-2t_{2}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}\left[1-\frac{1-\mathpzc{G}_{n}}{ \sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)\right\}\right]\times\] (53) \[\left\{\cos\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\cos\vec{k}\vec{b}_{n} +\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sum_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\mathpzc{\rho}( \vec{k}^{\prime})\cos\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}\] \[\mathcal{H}_{x}=+t_{1}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}^{\prime}\left[1+\frac{1- \mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}}{\sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\cos(2n \pi)\right\}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{n}\] \[\mathcal{H}_{x}=-2t_{2}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}\left[1-\frac{1-\mathpzc{G}_ {n}}{\sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)\right\}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\\(\mathcal{H}_{0}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) from Eq. (100) and \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\) from Eq. (104). Explicitly:

\[\begin{split}&\mathcal{H}_{0}=-2t_{2}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}\left[1- \frac{1-\zeta_{n}}{\sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi) \right\}\right]\times\\ &\left\{\cos\frac{\vec{q}_{z}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\cos\vec{k}\vec{b}_{ n}+\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{z}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sum_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\rho(\vec{k}^{ \prime})\cos\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}\\ &\mathcal{H}_{x}=+t_{1}\sum_{n}\omega^{\prime}_{n}\left[1-\frac{ \vec{q}^{\prime}_{n}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{n} \\ &\mathcal{H}_{y}=+t_{1}\sum_{n}\omega^{\prime}_{n}\left[1-\frac{ \vec{q}^{\prime}_{n}}{4}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{n}\right]\times\sin\vec{k}\vec{a}_{n} \\ &\mathcal{H}_{z}=-2t_{2}\sum_{n}\omega_{n}\left[1-\frac{1-\zeta_{ n}}{\sqrt{2}}\left\{\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)-\sum_{n}\cos(2n\pi)\right\}\right] \times\\ &\left\{-\sin\frac{\vec{q}_{z}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_ {n}+\cos\frac{\vec{q}_{z}\vec{b}_{n}}{2}\sum_{\vec{k}^{\prime}}\rho(\vec{k}^ {\prime})\sin\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{n}\right\}.\end{split} \tag{101}\]

Similarly, for \(\zeta^{\prime}\ll 1\) and \(\zeta\approx 1\) the Hamiltonian is found by combining the \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\) from Eq. (100) and \(\mathcal{H}_{0}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) from Eq. (104).

(102)

### Hamiltonian For spin \(S=3/2,5/2,\ldots\)

Hamiltonian for higher half-integer spin can be found by combining the aproximation made for \(S=1/2\) and for \(S=2,3,\ldots\) in App. C. For example for \(S=5/2\) one can write the term:

\[\begin{split}&\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{5/2}\\ &=\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+ \frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{2}\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2}.\end{split} \tag{103}\]

The integer power term (square) can be approximated as [see App. C]:

\[\begin{split}&\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{2}\approx 1-2\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2 \vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right).\end{split} \tag{104}\]

For \(\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\ll 1\) the square root term can be:

\[\begin{split}&\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{1/2}\approx 1-\frac{\zeta^{ \prime}_{n}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right).\end{split} \tag{105}\]

Multiplying Eq. (104) and (105) and keeping the terms only first order in \(\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\) we will have:

\[\begin{split}&\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{5/2}\\ &\approx 1-\frac{5\zeta^{\prime}_{n}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right).\end{split} \tag{106}\]

For arbitrary half-integer \(S\) Eq. (106) can be written as:

\[\begin{split}&\left[1-\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left( \vec{r}_{i}+\frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right)\right]^{(S-1/2)/2+1/2}\\ &\approx 1-S\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\left(\vec{r}_{i}+ \frac{\vec{a}_{n}}{2}\right).\end{split} \tag{107}\]

The same trick can be applied for finding the Hamiltonian for other cases of \(\zeta_{n}\) and \(\zeta^{\prime}_{n}\). The total Hamiltonian will be analogous to the Eqs. (104), (100), (101), and (102).

## Appendix E Chern number Calculations for Honeycomb bipartite lattice

### Spin \(S=1\)

We first calculate the Chern number for \(S=1\) using the Hamiltonian Eq. (4). As mentioned before the Chern number is calculated at values of momentum \(\pm\vec{K}\) when the condition \(\mathcal{H}_{x}=\mathcal{H}_{y}=0\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\neq 0\) is satisfied simultaneously [see Sec. 3.5.6 of Ref. [62]]. If we take \(\vec{q}_{1}=\left(2q_{1x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) and

