\(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(2q_{2x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\), then at the point \(\vec{K}=\left(\pm\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) the condition is satisfied identically. To see this, defining the three NN (\(a_{n}\)) and NNN (\(b_{n}\)) lattice vectors of the honeycomb lattice, in Eq. (4) for spin \(S=1\) we will get:

\[\mathcal{H}_{x}=+t_{1}\omega_{1}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G }_{1}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{1}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{1}\] \[\qquad\qquad+t_{1}\omega_{2}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G}_{ 2}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{2}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{2} \tag{15}\] \[\qquad\qquad+t_{1}\omega_{3}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G}_{ 3}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{3}\right]\times\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{3}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{y}=+t_{1}\omega_{1}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G }_{1}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{1}\right]\times\sin\vec{k}\vec{a}_{1}\] \[\qquad\qquad+t_{1}\omega_{2}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G}_{ 2}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{2}\right]\times\sin\vec{k}\vec{a}_{2} \tag{16}\] \[\qquad\qquad+t_{1}\omega_{3}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G}_{ 3}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1}\vec{a}_{3}\right]\times\sin\vec{k}\vec{a}_{3}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{z}=\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{1}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{1}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1 }\vec{b}_{1}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{1}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{1}+2\cos \vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{1}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left( \vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{1}\right\}\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{2}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{2}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1 }\vec{b}_{2}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{2}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{2}+2\cos \vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{2}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left( \vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{2}\right\}\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{3}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{3}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1 }\vec{b}_{3}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{3}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{3}+2\cos \vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{3}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left( \vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{3}\right\}. \tag{17}\]

In Eqs. (15), (16), and (17) we explicitly wrote the all three components of the \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\), \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\) and \(H_{z}\). We dropped the \(\mathcal{H}_{0}\) component as it does not play any role in determining the Chern number. Further one need to substitute the values of \(\vec{a}_{n}\), \(\vec{b}_{n}\), \(\vec{q}_{1}=\left(2q_{1x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\), \(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(2q_{2x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\), and \(\vec{K}=\left(\pm\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) in above equations. In Tab. 1 we have given the dot product of these values. Substituting these in Eqs. (15)(16)(16)(17) we will get:

\[\mathcal{H}_{x}=0, \tag{18}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{y}=\] \[\pm t_{1}\omega_{1}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{\mathcal{G}_{1}^{ \prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1x}\right]\mp t_{1}\omega_{2}^{\prime}\left[1-\frac{ \mathcal{G}_{2}^{\prime}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1x}\right]=0, \tag{19}\]

\[\mathcal{H}_{z}=\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{1}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{1}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\times\] \[\left\{\pm\sin\vec{q}_{2x}+2\cos q_{2x}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho \left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left(\pm\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right)\right\}\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{2}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\times \tag{20}\] \[\left\{\pm\sin\vec{q}_{2x}+2\cos q_{2x}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho \left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left(\pm\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{2}\right)\right\}\] \[-2t_{2}\omega_{3}\left[1+\frac{\mathcal{G}_{3}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\times\] \[\left\{+2\cos q_{2x}\sum_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime}\right) \sin\left(\pm\pi+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{3}\right)\right\}\]

We observe that all the terms of \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) are identically zero; the term involving \(\vec{a}_{1}\) and \(\vec{a}_{2}\) are zero as \(\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{1}=\cos\pi/2=0\) and \(\cos\vec{k}\vec{a}_{2}=\cos\pi/2=0\); the term involving \(a_{3}\) is zero as \(\omega_{3}=0\). For \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\) the summation of all three terms are zero; the term involving \(\vec{a}_{3}\) is zero as \(\sin\vec{k}a_{3}=\sin 0=0\); the terms involving \(\vec{a}_{1}\) and \(\vec{a}_{2}\) are opposite of each other, hence they cancel. The three terms of the \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) are not zero at \(\vec{K}\).

For \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) the term containing \(\vec{b}_{3}\) one can observe that both \(\rho(k^{\prime})\sim 0\) [see Fig. 14] and the term \(\sin\left(\pm\pi+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{3}\right)\sim 0\); hence, we can neglect this term due to at least an order of smallness compared to other two terms. The terms containing \(\vec{b}_{1}\) and \(\vec{b}_{2}\) controls the Chern number. In these, usually the terms containing summation over \(\vec{k}^{\prime}\) are negligible for most values of \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\); in Fig. 17 we showed the summation \(\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho(k^{\prime})\sin\left(\pi/2+k^{\prime}b_{1}\right) \ll 1\) at \(\vec{K}=\left(\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) for arbitrary values of the \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\). It should be kept in mind that at \(\vec{K}=\left(-\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) the summation \(\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho(k^{\prime})\sin\left(\pi/2+k^{\prime}b_{1}\right)\ll 1\) will be negative. Due to smallness of the summation, the term containing the same, won't have any effect on the Chern number, except at values where \(\sin q_{2x}\lesssim 2\left|\cos q_{2x}\right|\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho(k^{ \prime})\sin\left(\pi/2+k^{\prime}b_{1}\right)\), which is in the vicinity \(q_{2x}\sim 0\) and \(q_{2x}\sim\pm\pi/\sqrt{3}\). Away from these areas one can easily find the Chern number analytically. We will neglect the term containing the \(\rho(k^{\prime})\).

The values of \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\) and \(\omega_{n}\) are always positive and less than unity. Hence, they also won't have any effect on Chern number. Therefore the Chern number will be defined by only the term \(\sin\left(q_{2x}\right)\). The first Chern number is calculated by using the formula [See Eq. (42) of Ref. [62]]:

\[c_{1}= \tag{10}\] \[\frac{\text{sgn}\left\{\mathcal{H}_{z}\left(\vec{K}=\left(\pi/ \sqrt{3},0\right)\right)\right\}-\text{sgn}\left\{\mathcal{H}_{z}\left(\vec{ K}=\left(-\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\right)\right\}}{2}.\]

Explicitly the Chern number will be:

\[c_{1}=\text{sgn}\left[\sin\left(q_{2x}\right)\right]. \tag{11}\]

### Spin \(S=2,3,\ldots\)

The approximate Hamiltonian for higher spins is given in Eq. (101). If one compares the Hamiltonian Eq. (101) and (4) they almost look identical, except for extra \(S\) factors occurring in different places. Comparing \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) in Eq. (101) and (4) one will observe two changes: (i) \(\omega_{n}^{\prime}\) has a power of \(S\); (ii) \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}^{\prime}\) has an extra coefficient \(S\). However, the terms involving momentum \(\vec{k}\) remains same. The similar is true for \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\). For \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) one can observe five changes: (i) \(\omega_{n}\) has a power of \(S\); (ii) \(\mathpzc{G}_{n}\) has an extra coefficient \(S\); (iii) the angle of \(\sin\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\) has now an \(S\) coefficient: \(\sin\left(\vec{S}\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\right)\); (iv) the angle of \(\cos\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\) has now an \(S\) coefficient: \(\cos\left(\vec{S}\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{n}\right)\); (v) the last term has a coefficient \(2S\). However, as in \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{y}\) in \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) also the term containing momentum \(\vec{k}\) remains same.

The topological properties is found from the same condition \(\mathcal{H}_{x}=\mathcal{H}_{y}=0\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\neq 0\). The \(\mathcal{H}_{x}\) and \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) are zero at \(\vec{K}=\left(\pm\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\), \(\vec{q}_{1}=\left(2q_{1x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) and \(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(2q_{2x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) due to the same reasons as explained for the \(S=1\) case. For \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) the term containing \(\vec{b}_{3}\) can be neglected due to two order of smallness as explained for \(S=1\) case. Therefore the terms containing \(\vec{b}_{1}\) and \(\vec{b}_{2}\) which are non zero at \(\vec{K}\) controls the Chern number. For \(S=2\) case the Chern number can be described by Eq. (103): \(c_{1}=\text{sgn}\left[\sin\left(2q_{2x}\right)\right]\) for most part of the \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\) phase space. However with increasing spin \(S\) the expression will not work. Hence, a more general expression for Chern number is needed.

From Eq. (101) the terms of \(\mathcal{H}_{z}\) corresponding to \(\vec{b}_{1}\) and \(\vec{b}_{2}\) for arbitrary \(S\) is:

\[\mathcal{H}_{z}\approx \tag{12}\] \[-2_{2}\omega_{1}^{S}\left[1+\frac{Sq_{1}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \vec{b}_{1}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{1}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{1}+2S \cos S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{1}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime} \right)\sin\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{1}\right\}\right.\] \[-2_{2}\omega_{2}^{S}\left[1+\frac{Sq_{2}}{2}\cos 2\vec{q}_{1} \vec{b}_{2}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{2}\sin\vec{k}\vec{b}_{2}+2S \cos S\vec{q}_{2}\vec{b}_{2}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime} \right)\sin\left(\vec{k}+\vec{k}^{\prime}\right)\vec{b}_{2}\right\}.\]

Using \(\vec{K}=\left(\pm\pi/\sqrt{3},0\right)\), \(\vec{q}_{1}=\left(2q_{1x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) and \(\vec{q}_{2}=\left(2q_{2x}/\sqrt{3},0\right)\) and substituting the corresponding value from Tab. 1 in Eq. (12) we will get:

\[\mathcal{H}_{z}\approx \tag{13}\] \[-2_{2}\omega_{1}^{S}\left[1+\frac{Sq_{1}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin Sq_{2x}+2S\cos Sq_{2x}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho \left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left(\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right)\right\}\] \[-2_{2}\omega_{2}^{S}\left[1+\frac{Sq_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\times\] \[\left\{-\sin Sq_{2x}+2S\cos Sq_{2x}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho \left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left(\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{2}\right) \right\}.\]

Although the summation over \(\mathpzc{P}(k^{\prime})\) is always small as shown in Fig. 17, however, the whole term, \(2S\cos Sq_{2x}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\rho\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin\left( \pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right)\), might not be small compared to the previous term \(\sin Sq_{2x}\), due to the coefficient \(2S\) present in it. Hence now the effect of term containing \(\mathpzc{P}(k^{\prime})\) should also be included; for \(S=1\) it was neglected due to smallness. For ease of analysis we approximate the whole term with a small term \(\epsilon\) independent of \(q_{1x}\) and \(q_{2x}\):

\[\epsilon\equiv\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\mathpzc{P}\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin \left(\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right). \tag{14}\]

Explicitly we will write:

\[2S\cos Sq_{2x}\sum\limits_{k^{\prime}}\mathpzc{P}\left(k^{\prime}\right)\sin \left(\pi/2+\vec{k}^{\prime}\vec{b}_{1}\right)\equiv 2S\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}. \tag{15}\]

Value of \(\epsilon\) is positive for \(+\vec{K}\) and negative for \(-\vec{K}\). The value of \(\omega_{1}\) is always positive and less than unity [see Fig. 15], hence they wont affect the Chern number. Similarly, \(\mathpzc{g}_{n}\) is always positive and less than unity. However, its coefficients: (i) \(S\) may be large, (ii) \(\cos 2q_{1x}\) can take positive and negative values. Hence, now the term \(\left[1+\frac{Sq_{1}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right]\) can also affect the Chern number depending on \(S\) and \(q_{1x}\). All the above analysis is true for \(\vec{b}_{2}\) also. The Chern number using Eq. (10) will be:

\[c_{1}=\text{sign}\left[\left(1+\frac{Sg_{2}}{2}\cos 2q_{1x}\right)\left( \sin Sq_{2x}-2S\epsilon\cos Sq_{2x}\right)\right] \tag{12}\]

## Appendix F Neel type Skyrmion

Interestingly one can easily show the equivalence of the magnetic Skyrmions (Skx) to the spin texture given in Eq. (1) in specific limits. The magnetic Skx is represented as [see Eq. (8) of Ref. [107]]:

\[\vec{S}_{i,\text{Skx}}=\begin{bmatrix}S_{x}\\ S_{y}\\ S_{z}\end{bmatrix}=S\begin{bmatrix}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{ i}\right|\right)\left(\frac{x_{i}}{|\vec{r}_{i}|}\cos\gamma-\omega\frac{y_{i}}{| \vec{r}_{i}|}\sin\gamma\right)\\ \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\right)\left(\frac{x_{i}} {|\vec{r}_{i}|}\sin\gamma+\omega\frac{y_{i}}{|\vec{r}_{i}|}\sin\gamma\right) \\ \rho\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\right)\end{bmatrix}. \tag{13}\]

Here, \(\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\) is the length of the position vector at \(i\)-th site; \(x_{i}\) (\(y_{i}\)) is the \(x\) (\(y\)) component of the position vector \(\vec{r}_{i}\); \(r_{0}\) is the maximum length of the Skx. \(p\) is the polarity which represent the out of plane of the magnetization of the Skx; its value changes from \(+1\) (\(-1\)) at the center to the \(-1\) (\(+1\)) at the boundary. \(\omega\) is the vorticity which represent rotation of the magnetization around the \(z\)-axis; it takes multiples of \(2\pi\), \(\omega=0,\pm 1,\pm 2,\dots\). \(\gamma\) is the helicity which represent the rotation of the magnetization around the \(i\)-th site. If we substitute \(\gamma=0\), \(\omega=1\) and \(\rho=1\) in Eq. (13); and substituting \(x_{i}=\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\cos\phi\), \(y_{i}=\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\sin\phi\) we will get:

\[\vec{S}_{i,\text{Skx}}=\begin{bmatrix}S_{x}\\ S_{y}\\ S_{z}\end{bmatrix}=S\begin{bmatrix}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_ {i}\right|\right)\cos\phi\\ \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\right)\sin\phi\\ \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\right)\end{bmatrix}. \tag{14}\]

Physically it represents the Neel-type Skx [107]. In Eq. (14) one needs to map \(\phi\to q_{2}\tan 2(r_{i})\) and \(\frac{\pi}{|r_{0}|}\left|\vec{r}_{i}\right|\to q_{1}|\vec{r}_{i}|\) to see the similarity with Eq. (1). In contrast to Eq. (1) now the spin modulating vectors become scalars. Besides for a single Skx (not Skx lattice) the \(q_{1}\) and \(q_{2}\) can not be chosen arbitrary, they are fixed:

\[q_{2}=1,\quad q_{1}=\frac{\pi}{r_{0}}. \tag{15}\]

In Fig. 18 we plotted the Skx spin texture using Eq. (14).

